This is a simple example of the patch we used, though this breaks other cluster's use cases because it assumes that there are no intermediary certs/different CA certs so some logic has to change there to support those. But I think shows the general idea of what the config options and the Authenticator/Verifier would look like, stripping the verify() calls and using the VerifiedChains from the already verified connection (this is a patch we created on top of k8s 1.17.3 FYI):
```diff
diff --git a/cmd/kubelet/app/server.go b/cmd/kubelet/app/server.go
index 8a705bb139a..d63da0234fe 100644
--- a/cmd/kubelet/app/server.go
+++ b/cmd/kubelet/app/server.go
@@ -1008,7 +1008,7 @@ func InitializeTLS(kf *options.KubeletFlags, kc *kubeletconfiginternal.KubeletCo
 		// Specify allowed CAs for client certificates
 		tlsOptions.Config.ClientCAs = clientCAs
 		// Populate PeerCertificates in requests, but don't reject connections without verified certificates
-		tlsOptions.Config.ClientAuth = tls.RequestClientCert
+		tlsOptions.Config.ClientAuth = tls.VerifyClientCertIfGiven
 	}
 
 	return tlsOptions, nil
diff --git a/staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/authentication/request/x509/x509.go b/staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/authentication/request/x509/x509.go
